#
# The same undecidable `NOT IN`, used as a filter predicate rather than as a `when` condition.
#
# Both roles carry `ClauseRole::Gate` -- `eval_when_clause` and the three filter sites in
# `eval_context.rs` all pass it -- so a repair keyed on the role alone reaches both, and the two
# absorb a failure in different ways. A `when` condition that fails makes the rule inapplicable; a
# filter predicate that fails selects nothing, and an assertion over an empty selection SKIPs. Both
# end at exit 0 with the body unchecked, so both need the same answer.
#
# This file is the canary for the direction that repair must NOT take. `docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md` and
# `incomparable_membership_notice` record five aws-guard-rules-registry rules that use `NOT IN` inside
# a filter predicate to catch a `!Ref`-shaped value, where failing closed would make the filter select
# fewer resources and turn a reported violation into a pass. Those rules are untouched, and the
# distinction is mechanical rather than a judgment call: `Unanswerable` splits a comparison the engine
# abandoned from a refusal to compare two kinds, and only the first fails a gate closed. A kind
# mismatch still records `NotComparable` exactly as before and still answers a plain FAIL.
#
# The registry is what makes that split non-negotiable rather than tidy. Rule authors write a value
# against both spellings it might carry -- `ScanOnPush == 'False' OR ScanOnPush == false` -- and rely
# on the pairing that does not apply answering "no" rather than "unknown". Measured: treating every
# undecided comparison as unknown moves 143 rules of the pinned corpus off their expectations, against
# 0 failed rules at the merge-base.
#
# Measured on this template before the fix: exit 0, the filter selected nothing, and `MustBeTrue` was
# never compared.
#
rule unrelated_violation {
    Region == 'no-such-region-zzz'
}

rule guarded {
    Resources.*[ Properties.Cat NOT IN [/(?!x)((a+)+)b/] ].Properties.MustBeTrue == true
}
